format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: inside-the-atom

lesson isotopes
title: Isotopes
minutes: 4
xp: 50
teaches: u1-isotope-definition
teaches: u1-isotope-not-radioactive
relies_on: u1-particles
relies_on: u1-atomic-number

description:
    Atoms of one element always have the same number of protons, but they can have different numbers of neutrons. Those versions are called isotopes. You will see that an isotope is not automatically radioactive.

cite ref-openstax-chem-ch2
supports: Isotope definition and mass number.

cite ref-epa-radioactive-decay
supports: Some forms of an element are stable and others unstable.

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Two carbons, two weights
figure: isotope-pair
figure_at: 2

body:
    Here is a puzzle. Two atoms are both carbon, but one is heavier. How? Carbon atoms always have 6 protons, but the number of neutrons can differ: carbon-12 has 6 and carbon-14 has 8. The protons decide the element, so both are carbon. These versions of one element are called [[isotope|isotopes]].

card c03
kind: sim
type: atom-builder
scored: 0
task: Make carbon-14 from carbon-12 by adding 2 neutrons.
hint: Carbon-12 has 6 neutrons. Tap + two times. The protons did not change, so it is still carbon.

